Explainable AI-Based Wearable Heart Disease Risk Prediction and Remote Monitoring System

Abstract
Cardiovascular Disease (CVD) remains one of the leading causes of mortality worldwide, making early detection and continuous monitoring essential for improving patient outcomes. Conventional heart disease prediction systems primarily rely on clinical data and often operate as black-box models, limiting their interpretability and reducing clinician trust. This project proposes an Explainable AI-Based Wearable Heart Disease Risk Prediction and Remote Monitoring System that integrates wearable IoT technology with machine learning to provide accurate, transparent, and real-time cardiovascular risk assessment. A wearable device built using ESP32, MAX30102, and AD8232 sensors continuously collects physiological parameters such as heart rate, blood oxygen saturation (SpO₂), and ECG signals. The collected data, along with clinical information, is processed using an Artificial Neural Network (ANN) model to predict heart disease risk. To improve transparency, SHAP (SHapley Additive Explanations) is incorporated to explain the model's predictions. The prediction results are displayed through a cloud-based dashboard, enabling healthcare professionals to remotely monitor patients and receive timely alerts during abnormal health conditions. The proposed framework aims to enhance prediction accuracy, improve clinician trust, and support continuous healthcare monitoring.

Keywords
Heart Disease Prediction, Explainable AI, Internet of Things (IoT), Wearable Devices, Artificial Neural Network, SHAP, Remote Patient Monitoring.

Introduction

Cardiovascular Disease (CVD) is one of the most significant global health concerns and is responsible for millions of deaths every year. Early diagnosis and continuous monitoring play a vital role in reducing the severity of cardiovascular complications and improving patient survival rates. With the rapid growth of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Internet of Things (IoT) technologies, intelligent healthcare systems have become increasingly capable of supporting disease prediction and remote patient monitoring.

Existing heart disease prediction systems mainly utilize machine learning algorithms trained on clinical datasets to estimate disease risk. Although these models often achieve high prediction accuracy, many operate as black-box systems, making it difficult for healthcare professionals to understand the reasoning behind the predictions. This lack of transparency limits the adoption of AI in critical healthcare applications. Moreover, most existing systems rely on periodic clinical assessments and do not support continuous monitoring using wearable devices.

Recent advancements in wearable technology have enabled continuous collection of physiological parameters such as heart rate, blood oxygen saturation (SpO₂), and electrocardiogram (ECG) signals. However, many wearable solutions are limited to displaying sensor readings and do not provide intelligent heart disease risk prediction with interpretable results. There is a growing need for an integrated healthcare framework that combines wearable sensing, machine learning, Explainable AI, and remote monitoring.

To address these challenges, this project proposes an Explainable AI-Based Wearable Heart Disease Risk Prediction and Remote Monitoring System. The proposed system uses an ESP32-based wearable prototype integrated with MAX30102 and AD8232 sensors to collect real-time physiological data. An Artificial Neural Network (ANN) is employed to predict heart disease risk, while SHAP (SHapley Additive Explanations) is used to explain the prediction results. A cloud-based dashboard enables healthcare professionals to monitor patient health remotely and receive alerts when abnormal conditions are detected. By integrating wearable IoT technology with Explainable AI, the proposed system aims to improve prediction transparency, support early diagnosis, and enhance clinical decision-making.
